   "displayName": "GO Term",
   "fullName": "GeneQuestions.GenesByGoTerm",
   "description": "Find genes based on the Gene Ontology (GO) Term(s) or ID(s) assigned to them. GO annotations \n           were either provided by the sequencing and annotation\n           centers or inferred based on similarity to\n           protein domains from the\n           <a href=\"http://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/\">InterPro</a> databases.<br><br>\n           \n           \n           The Gene Ontology standardizes the representation of gene and gene product attributes \n           across species and databases.  The ontologies are a controlled vocabulary of terms for describing \n           the molecular function, biological process and subcellular location of a gene product. This search \n           returns genes with the GO Term or ID that you specify, as well as genes with terms that are\n           specializations of your term (is-a).  You may use '*' as a wild card character. For a list of \n           GO terms, try the <a href=\"http://amigo.geneontology.org/amigo/landing\">AmiGO</a>\n           browser. <br><br>\n           \n\t   When using the GO Subsets parameter selecting GO Slim generic will limit the search to GO terms that are part of the GO Slim generic ontology subset. \n\t   To run on all available GO terms please select All GO terms.<br><br> \n           \n           When using the 'GO Term or GO ID' parameter, begin typing a GO Term or ID \n            see a list of all possible GO categories that match what you have typed. Then choose a GO category from \n            the list. You can choose multiple domains using CTRL- or CMD-click when choosing the domains.<br><br>\n            \n            When using the 'Free Text (use'*' for wildcard)' parameter, type the text term you wish to find and include an asterisk wildcard \n            for ambiguous portions of your text term.  (*ase, kin*, *kinase*)  The search will query for all terms or IDs whose \n            names, accessions or descriptions contain your text. \n            <br><br>",
